// Eventhouse retrieval probe (ADR 0030 fork) — decides whether semantic
// search can run inside the Data Agent via KQL, where there is NO mirror
// layer between the agent and the data.
//
// HOW TO RUN (learned live 2026-08-08): KQL executes ONE dot-command
// at a time — put the cursor INSIDE a command (no selection) and hit
// Run; running a multi-command selection fails (the .ingest ran before
// the .create existed -> TableNotFoundException). The section-3 search
// query is a single statement and runs as one unit.
//
// Two syntax notes up front:
// - The embedding plugin is `ai_embeddings` (preview). Its output
//   column is named <input_column>_embeddings (verified live
//   2026-08-08 via getschema: input t -> t_embeddings). If any invocation
//   below errors on syntax, the canonical reference is the official
//   tutorial: learn.microsoft.com/fabric/real-time-intelligence/vector-database-eventhouse
// - KQL uses cosine SIMILARITY (higher = closer). Our SQL probe measured
//   DISTANCE (lower = closer). The section-3 SQL results translate to:
//   true match ~0.51 similarity, non-matches ~0.37. Threshold ~0.45.

// ===== 0. Portal prereqs (before any KQL) ============================
// a. Workspace -> + New item -> Eventhouse (name: "semantic_catalog"). A KQL
//    database is created inside it automatically.
// b. Azure portal -> the aivia resource -> Access control (IAM) ->
//    Add role assignment -> role "Cognitive Services OpenAI User" ->
//    assign to founder@aiviaapp.com. (The plugin calls Azure OpenAI AS
//    YOU — "impersonation" — so YOUR account needs permission on the
//    resource. No key is used anywhere in this probe.)
// c. Callout policy — Eventhouse must be allowed to call Azure OpenAI.
//    In the KQL database query editor, run:
//      .alter-merge cluster policy callout @'[{"CalloutType":"azure_openai","CalloutUriRegex":"aivia\\.openai\\.azure\\.com","CanCall":true}]'
//    If that errors on permissions, look for the callout setting in the
//    Eventhouse item's settings UI — this is the step most likely to
//    need iteration; capture any error verbatim.

// ===== 1. Table + seed rows ==========================================
.create table semantic_catalog (
    node_id:string, name:string, business_name:string,
    description:string, emb:dynamic
)

.ingest inline into table semantic_catalog <|
canonical:reporting.USP_ED_Sepsis,reporting.USP_ED_Sepsis,ED Sepsis Screening,"Measures pediatric patients under 21 days old presenting to the emergency department with valid sepsis screening scores and recorded blood pressure measurements.",
canonical:reports.USP_Severe_Sepsis,reports.USP_Severe_Sepsis,Severe Sepsis Episodes,"Reports management timelines of unique severe sepsis episodes, including arrival times and care event metrics.",
transform:reporting.USP_ED_Sepsis:ED_Readmit,Base_Pop_ED_Readmit,,"Patient encounters readmitted to the emergency department within 24 hours of a prior visit.",
transform:reporting.USP_ED_Sepsis:BPA,BPA,,"Timeline of clinical alerts and actions taken for each patient encounter, detailing alert statuses and overridden comments.",
canonical:reporting.USP_IP_SepsisDates,reporting.USP_IP_SepsisDates,Sepsis Reporting Calendar,"A filtered list of fiscal dates relevant to sepsis cases within a specified date range.",

// ===== 2. Embed the rows (one-time; writes emb) ======================
// Uses YOUR identity via ";impersonate" — needs prereq 0b + 0c.
.set-or-replace semantic_catalog <|
    semantic_catalog
    | extend combined = strcat(name, " | ", coalesce(business_name, ""), " | ", description)
    | evaluate ai_embeddings(combined,
        'https://aivia.openai.azure.com/openai/deployments/text-embedding-3-small/embeddings?api-version=2024-06-01;impersonate')
    | project node_id, name, business_name, description, emb = combined_embeddings

// Storage optimization (optional but documented best practice):
.alter column semantic_catalog.emb policy encoding @'{"Type":"Vector16"}'  // JSON form; shorthand rejected live

// ===== 3. Verify the search works when YOU run it ====================
// (If this fails, stop — the problem is setup, not the agent. A callout
// or 403 error here means prereq 0b or 0c, not a platform verdict.)
let q = toscalar(
    print t = "patients who came back to the ER"
    | evaluate ai_embeddings(t,
        'https://aivia.openai.azure.com/openai/deployments/text-embedding-3-small/embeddings?api-version=2024-06-01;impersonate')
    | project t_embeddings);
semantic_catalog
| extend similarity = series_cosine_similarity(q, emb)
| top 3 by similarity desc
| project node_id, name, business_name, similarity
// Expect Base_Pop_ED_Readmit first, similarity ~0.5; others ~0.37.

// ===== 4. Data Agent part (portal, not KQL) ==========================
// a. New throwaway Data Agent "EH Probe"; ONLY source: this KQL
//    database; tick semantic_catalog in the schema selection.
// b. Agent instructions (paste):
//      To find catalog entries matching a user's topic, embed the CORE
//      CONCEPT of their question (short noun phrase) using the
//      ai_embeddings plugin with the aivia connection string, then rank
//      by series_cosine_similarity descending. Report how many rows
//      score above 0.45 before listing the top matches. Similarity is
//      relative closeness, never a probability. If nothing scores above
//      0.45, say the catalog has nothing sufficiently related.
// c. ONE example pair:
//    Q: "what do we have about cancelled appointments?"
//    KQL:
//      let q = toscalar(print t = "cancelled appointments"
//        | evaluate ai_embeddings(t, 'https://aivia.openai.azure.com/openai/deployments/text-embedding-3-small/embeddings?api-version=2024-06-01;impersonate')
//        | project t_embeddings);
//      semantic_catalog
//      | extend similarity = series_cosine_similarity(q, emb)
//      | top 10 by similarity desc
//      | project node_id, name, business_name, description, similarity
//    >>> If the validator REJECTS this pair, screenshot the error —
//    >>> that alone is a verdict (same wall as SQL: validation layer).
// d. THE PROBE: ask "anything about newborns screened for sepsis in
//    the ER?" — open run steps and check:
//      [ ] generated KQL contains ai_embeddings          -> steering OK
//      [ ] it EXECUTED and returned rows                 -> ENGINE PASS
//      [ ] top row is the ED Sepsis metric               -> quality OK
// e. VERDICT -> record in ADR 0030:
//      PASS: Eventhouse is the semantic-retrieval architecture
//      FAIL at validation: agent's KQL validator rejects the plugin ->
//        the wall is the validation layer across engines; Azure AI
//        Search becomes the primary candidate
//      FAIL at execution (callout/permission): fix 0b/0c and retry
//        before concluding anything
// f. Delete the throwaway agent; keep/delete the Eventhouse per verdict.

// ===== 5. SQL-DB disambiguation (2 min, architect follow-up) =========
// Settles WHICH wall the SQL probe hit. In the ORIGINAAL probe SQL
// database, open its **SQL analytics endpoint** (the read-only twin
// item) and run:
//   SELECT COLUMN_NAME FROM INFORMATION_SCHEMA.COLUMNS
//   WHERE TABLE_NAME = 'semantic_catalog';
//   -- emb present?  -> mirror does NOT drop vector columns
//   -- emb absent?   -> mirror-drop confirmed (second wall real)
// then:
//   SELECT AI_GENERATE_EMBEDDINGS('hi' USE MODEL aivia_embeddings);
//   -- parses/runs?  -> functions exist on the endpoint
//   -- unrecognized? -> endpoint lacks AI functions (as researched)
// Record both outcomes in ADR 0030's probe verdict.
